Shows every pin of the board's chip: which driver or program holds it (or that the board wires
it to something fixed), what the IO_MUX and the GPIO matrix route through it, its direction and
pulls, and its level, live, ten times a second while the window is shown.

Pins lists them all, sortable, by class or by connector; choose one for everything about it
(its IO_MUX functions, the matrix signals in and out, drive, strapping). Board draws the board's
connectors (UEXT, EXT1) as their headers are laid out, each pin coloured by who holds it with
its level inside, and then every pad of the chip; click one to see it on Pins.

It only reads: nothing is taken or changed. It reads /dev/gpiochip0, the same device Linux's
GPIO tools use.

Needs a BMDS newer than 0.4.0 (gpio_ctl).
